  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Showers and perhaps a thunderstorm are possible \nover the southern tier of PA today and this evening. Dry \nconditions for Friday before an unsettled weekend.\n\nClouds are trying to clear out over the north, so we've added\npatchy fog to the river valleys there and in the central\nmountains as we/they may clear up better than the south by\nmorning. \n\nThe cold front won't likely pass through until the evening, so\nthere will be additional SHRA today with up to 60 pct in \nSomerset Co. A stray shower (no thunder) could also affect \nWarren/McKean Cos as the cool air aloft helps create some very \nweak instability along the NY border and the cold front passes.\nHowever, instability over the CWA will be low (<500J/kg in the\nfar SW), so only some isolated thunder is expected - and only in\nthe Laurels. The risk for heavy rain has decreased markedly \nthrough the last 48 hrs of model runs, and the WPC ERO MRGL risk\narea won't touch PA anymore (stays to our S) with the new \nguidance to be issued soon. The most-possible time for rain will\nbe the afternoon and early evening as some of the weak \nconvection moves in from the SW. The dewpoints don't come down\nuntil the front arrives (mid-late morning N, close to sunset in\nthe S). So, it may still feel a little muggy today, esp from\nI-80 southward with 65-70F Tds all day long. \n\nHigh pressure builds behind the cold front, giving way to a dry\nand mainly sunny Friday. AM valley fog is almost a given. The\nextent of the fog is a question, but we'll paint it in all the\nnormal nrn valleys at this point. Low level moisture lurking \njust south of the Mason/Dixon line Fri night will advance NE \nacross the state on Saturday as SFC high pressure moves well off\nthe Mid Atlantic and Southern New England Coast. This will lead\nto some showers spreading NE across the region with a few PM \nTSRA possible Saturday and/or Sunday afternoons, especially over\nthe western mtns. A more-active (wetter) pattern is in store \nfor Mon-Tues. But, the flat upper ridge over the southern half \nof the CONUS will make it difficult for deep moisture to make it\ninto PA ahead of any shots of atmos forcing. The week could \nend dry as the upper ridge pumps up over the central CONUS.",
